WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.822 --> 00:00:04.030
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello again.

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:06.240
I'm Dustin Kirkland, a
product manager at Google,

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.970
and I'm here with one of my
colleagues, David Oppenheimer.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.650
He's a software engineer working
on multi-tenancy in Kubernetes

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:13.240
at Google.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.450
David, I've got a couple
of questions for you

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.720
about multi-tenancy
in Kubernetes.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.010
I was at KubeCon a
year ago in Berlin,

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.410
and multi-tenancy
was quite the topic.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.750
What have you heard
over the last year

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.030
in terms of customer
and user demands

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.960
around multi-tenancy
in Kubernetes?

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.310
DAVID OPPENHEIMER:
Yeah, so a year ago

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.430
it was very popular for people
to just run lots of clusters

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.970
if they had different users or
applications who they wanted

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.630
to isolate from one another.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.650
And over the last year,
we've either introduced

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.190
or brought to a greater level
of maturity, like beta or GA,

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.070
a bunch of features in
open-source Kubernetes

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.052
that enable multi-tenancy
within a cluster.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.760
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Within
a single cluster.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.890
So that's a big change,
because I've seen this as well.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.040
Every team, every department
gets their own Kubernetes

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:58.540
cluster.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:01.230
They're completely
separate management planes.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.980
So you're saying that as
of Kubernetes 1.10 now,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.897
I should be able to run multiple
tenants in a single cluster?

00:01:07.897 --> 00:01:09.480
DAVID OPPENHEIMER:
Yeah, that's right.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:13.620
So some of the features that we
have that are mature now are--

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.950
the most well-known one
is RBAC for authorization.

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:20.190
So you can put very
fine-grained controls

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:23.610
over which users or groups
of users or applications

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.860
can create and update and delete
which API resources on the API

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:29.880
server.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.820
And so that kind of gives
you control plane isolation.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:35.070
And then we also
have other features

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.860
that provide isolation on the
node or at the container level.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.500
For example, we have
pod security policy,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.760
which lets an administrator
say, for example, no privilege

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.120
containers in this cluster
or in this namespace,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:51.330
or you can't run any containers
as root in this namespace,

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.660
or these users can't do that.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.930
So gives you pretty
fine-grained control over what

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.280
privileges a container's
running in the cluster.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.110
And then another policy that's
kind of at the node level

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.690
is network policy,
which controls

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:10.229
which pods can communicate with
which other pods so that you

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:13.440
can, for example, isolate
namespaces from one another,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.817
or say that you have part
of your application in one

00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:17.400
namespace and part
of your application

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.300
in another namespace.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.760
And the pods that
constitute that application

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.260
should be able to
talk to each other

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.210
across that namespace
boundary, but not

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.160
with other namespaces
in the cluster.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:28.230
And so you can kind of
partition your cluster

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.930
based on the groups of
users and the applications

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.590
and the topology of
the application using

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:35.280
network policy.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.510
So between those three features,
the RBAC and the network policy

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:42.270
and the pod security
policy, we've

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.130
kind of met a lot of
the needs that people

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.730
have for multi-tenancy for
running enterprises or software

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:50.790
as a service where you
want to provide isolation

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:54.224
between untrusted users
sharing a cluster.

00:02:54.224 --> 00:02:56.640
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So now it's
realistic for Coke and Pepsi,

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:59.720
as separate tenants, to
run in the same cluster.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.410
That feels reasonable
at this point.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.520
DAVID OPPENHEIMER:
Well, so Coke and Pepsi

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.660
is kind of going to one
extreme of the spectrum.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.460
And so one of the things that
people have asked a lot about--

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:11.160
and actually there's been
just very recent technology

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:12.870
developed around
solving that problem--

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:18.020
is kind of a hypervisor level
of isolation between containers.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.700
So when I was talking about
pod security policy and network

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.650
policy earlier,
those are kind of

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.075
based on the container
isolation in Docker

00:03:27.075 --> 00:03:28.950
or whatever runtime
you're using,

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.660
and also what your
network provider

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.790
can provide for
the network policy.

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:38.100
But multiple
containers on a node

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.950
are still sharing
the same kernel.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.736
And so if you want the
level of protection

00:03:43.736 --> 00:03:45.360
that you need, say,
for Coke and Pepsi,

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.240
then you should use
something like gVisor,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:50.850
which was announced
today, or yesterday, I

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.340
guess, from Google,
which allows you

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.230
to have kind of the hypervisor
level of security and isolation

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.200
between containers that
are sharing a cluster.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.300
And so I think that for the
Coke and Pepsi use case,

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.840
now it's available, now
it's possible to do that.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:08.420
But that's a very
recent development.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:09.000
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That's great.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.580
And in terms of what's coming
next, can you give us, maybe,

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.850
a brief taste of
where multi-clusters

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.089
and multi-tenancy is
headed in Kubernetes?

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:18.089
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: Sure.

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.040
There's kind of two
areas where there's

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.320
ongoing work for multi-tenancy.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.390
One of them is
related to policies,

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:29.169
so making it easier for people
to specify these policies.

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:31.710
Today you kind of have to be a
bit of an expert in Kubernetes

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:34.647
in order to know how to set
up the multi-tenancy policies

00:04:34.647 --> 00:04:35.730
in a multi-tenant cluster.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.790
So some of the work
that's going on

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.570
is kind of towards unifying and
making it simpler to specify

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.230
the different policies.

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:47.250
And then we also
have work that's

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:52.020
kind of not related to policies,
but more related to isolation.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:56.250
So for example, improving
the isolation between tenants

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.490
in the control plane.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.480
So for example, different
users can't DOS each other

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.420
by overloading the scheduler,
overloading the API server.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:07.510
So giving the controllers
in the other components

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:09.670
of the Kubernetes
control plane some notion

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.430
of how much of the
control plane resources

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:15.760
each tenant is using so
that it can kind of fairly

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.530
share the resources
across those tenants,

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.540
or at least prevent them
from DOSing each other,

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.510
and ensure that all the
tenants can make progress

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:25.510
even if one of them is trying
to impose a lot of load

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:27.130
on the scheduler
or the API server,

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:29.140
or some other piece
of the control plane.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.430
So that's one of the key things
that we're working on now

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.064
to improving the
multi-tenancy story.

00:05:35.064 --> 00:05:35.980
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.145
It's super impressive how
far we've come in a year.

00:05:38.145 --> 00:05:40.520
And I'm really looking forward
to where we're going next.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:41.500
David, thank you.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.670
DAVID OPPENHEIMER: Yeah,
great talking to you.

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.220
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah, thanks.

